{
  "an10.54:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 10.54 ",
  "an10.54:0.2": "6. Your Own Mind ",
  "an10.54:0.3": "Serenity ",
  "an10.54:1.1": "“Mendicants, if a mendicant isn’t skilled in encompassing another’s mind, then they should train themselves: ‘I will be skilled in encompassing my own mind.’ ",
  "an10.54:1.2": "",
  "an10.54:2.1": "And how is a mendicant skilled in encompassing their own mind? ",
  "an10.54:2.2": "Suppose there was a woman or man who was young, youthful, and fond of adornments, and they check their own reflection in a clean bright mirror or a clear bowl of water. If they see any dirt or blemish there, they’d try to remove it. ",
  "an10.54:2.3": "But if they don’t see any dirt or blemish there, they’re happy with that, as they’ve got all they wished for: ",
  "an10.54:2.4": "‘How fortunate that I’m clean!’ ",
  "an10.54:2.5": "In the same way, checking is very helpful for a mendicant’s skillful qualities. ",
  "an10.54:2.6": "‘Do I have internal serenity of heart or not? Do I have the higher wisdom of discernment of principles or not?’ ",
  "an10.54:3.1": "Suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows this: ",
  "an10.54:3.2": "‘I have serenity but not discernment.’ Grounded on serenity, they should practice meditation to get discernment. ",
  "an10.54:3.3": "After some time they have both serenity and discernment. ",
  "an10.54:4.1": "But suppose that, upon checking, a mendicant knows this: ",
  "an10.54:4.2": "‘I have discernment but not serenity.’ Grou